//! Shared by the SP1 guest and host: the canonical statement/witness codec,
//! the exact hash framing of `veritor.protocol.merkle`, the pinned gate
//! semantics of `veritor.core.gates` and `veritor.core.int8`, and the
//! [`kernel`] -- the verification kernel that decides one batch of proof
//! obligations behind a single acceptance boundary ([`kernel::verify`]).
//!
//! Everything here mirrors a Python module byte for byte; the cross-language
//! vectors in `tests/vectors.json` are generated by the Python side
//! (`tests/veritor/protocol/proofs/test_vectors.py`) and asserted here.

//!
//! # Diagnostic features (never sound, never a result)
//!
//! `unsound-skip-authentication` makes the checker skip the leaf hashing and
//! the path folding; `unsound-skip-path-fold` keeps the leaf hashing and skips
//! only the folding.  They exist to measure the cycle floor of the
//! authentication pass and nothing else: a build with either feature accepts
//! witnesses that open nothing.  Such a build prints [`DIAGNOSTIC_BANNER`] at
//! start and commits [`VERDICT_ACCEPT`] `= 0xEE` / [`VERDICT_REJECT`] `= 0xEF`
//! instead of `1` / `0`, so no verifier that checks for `1` can mistake its
//! output for a verdict.  The host reports the byte as `unsound`.

